<?xml version="1.0"?>
<events>
	<!-- ONAT: SILENT CROWD - the biggest crowd is NOT where the demand is. It tests whether a
	     policy follows the REQUESTS (and the services they need) or only the people, and whether
	     UAVs of DIFFERENT services can be brought together - which a stream needs.
	       - TOWN_SQUARE (VIDEO_APP, service 0): the whole map comes to the middle (attraction radius
	         450 m, 90% of the users, so ~460 people at 800 users on 100 m) but each of them asks only
	         once in ~13 minutes: about 0.6 requests/s in total, a fraction of one UAV. A crowd that
	         pulls every service-blind policy (they follow users) and hardly needs anything: one UAV
	         hosting service 0 at its center is enough.
	       - CHEMICAL_LEAK (RESCUE_STREAM = DETECT -> (MAP, VIDEO) -> ALERT, all 4 services, 300-900 s), in
	         the south-east corner, far from the crowd (520 m): ~80 people at 800 users, but every request is a
	         stream and needs a UAV of EACH of the 4 services within 100 m of it at the same time, or it
	         fails (30 s timeout). The 4 UAVs have to be different ones, stacked on the same spot -
	         exactly what a policy that spreads UAVs apart by their positions works against, and what
	         "UAVs of different services may stack" (SERVICE_APF's overlap-aware repulsion) is for.
	       - POWER_OUTAGE (TRAFFIC_STREAM = MAP -> ALERT, 1000-1600 s), in the opposite, north-west corner
	         (1050 m from the leak): after a 100 s pause the demand jumps across the whole map and
	         changes its services: only MAP and ALERT UAVs are wanted there, so the VIDEO UAV may stay
	         with the square and the DETECT UAVs have nothing to do. A UAV needs ~70 s to make the trip at
	         15 m/s, longer than service_demand_window (60 s): a policy can only be early if it
	         moves before the demand is fully there - try service_demand_lookahead (an oracle) to see how
	         much of the loss is that delay.
	     What to look for: the streams' failure rate per policy (the stream plots), UAVs hosting the
	     missing services appearing at the corner, and the VIDEO requests of the square still being
	     served (no UAV hosting service 0 left within reach of it is the price of an emergency elsewhere).
	     At 800 users the leak makes ~0.8 streams/s and the outage ~0.8 streams/s, each needing ~0.65 of a
	     UAV of every service involved; at 200 users there are only 13 and 21 people at the corners.
	     All times in simulation seconds (1800 s in total). -->

	<event name="TOWN_SQUARE">
		<start_time>200</start_time>
		<end_time>1700</end_time>
		<x_pos>500</x_pos>
		<y_pos>500</y_pos>
		<radius>100</radius>
		<attraction_radius>450</attraction_radius>
		<participation>0.9</participation>
		<request>VIDEO_APP</request>
		<poisson_interarrival>800</poisson_interarrival>
	</event>

	<event name="CHEMICAL_LEAK">
		<start_time>300</start_time>
		<end_time>900</end_time>
		<x_pos>870</x_pos>
		<y_pos>130</y_pos>
		<radius>80</radius>
		<attraction_radius>250</attraction_radius>
		<participation>0.9</participation>
		<request>RESCUE_STREAM</request>
		<poisson_interarrival>100</poisson_interarrival>
	</event>

	<event name="POWER_OUTAGE">
		<start_time>1000</start_time>
		<end_time>1600</end_time>
		<x_pos>130</x_pos>
		<y_pos>870</y_pos>
		<radius>80</radius>
		<attraction_radius>250</attraction_radius>
		<participation>0.9</participation>
		<request>TRAFFIC_STREAM</request>
		<poisson_interarrival>60</poisson_interarrival>
	</event>
</events>
